[[:db/retract 17592186162365 :question/answer-fields 17592186159392] {:db/id 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d906fadc-a9c3-53a2-ad5f-49a5ac38ab8d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d906fadc-a9c3-53a2-ad5f-49a5ac38ab8d" :question/id #uuid "d906fadc-a9c3-53a2-ad5f-49a5ac38ab8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139532" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $11$ and $4,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $11,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=11\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{11}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-31f990c5-9445-506b-958c-e6c3b52764dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-31f990c5-9445-506b-958c-e6c3b52764dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "31f990c5-9445-506b-958c-e6c3b52764dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ddda3679-2b94-54f4-b126-2775a43b783a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5a97219a-abbc-57e2-b1d2-b514ebfcc818" "answer-7ae8a743-3acf-5070-9428-27ae75be7fae" "answer-679d7be2-afd4-56e4-a222-6d038f0fed97" "answer-ddda3679-2b94-54f4-b126-2775a43b783a" "answer-540c0dc8-bcd9-54af-927a-97418f253efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a97219a-abbc-57e2-b1d2-b514ebfcc818" :answer/id #uuid "5a97219a-abbc-57e2-b1d2-b514ebfcc818" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ae8a743-3acf-5070-9428-27ae75be7fae" :answer/id #uuid "7ae8a743-3acf-5070-9428-27ae75be7fae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-679d7be2-afd4-56e4-a222-6d038f0fed97" :answer/id #uuid "679d7be2-afd4-56e4-a222-6d038f0fed97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddda3679-2b94-54f4-b126-2775a43b783a" :answer/id #uuid "ddda3679-2b94-54f4-b126-2775a43b783a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-540c0dc8-bcd9-54af-927a-97418f253efe" :answer/id #uuid "540c0dc8-bcd9-54af-927a-97418f253efe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163315 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose we are given the matrix\n\n![](images/18/18f57c05cb79918e17401427e3419648791562a5d38116582e0d396e20db1069.svg)\n\nThen, the corresponding linear transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x \\mapsto A \\mathbf x\n$$\n\nmaps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf x \\, | \\, \\|\\mathbf x\\|=1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb R^2$ onto an ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/c1/c163f7714d0e2045db37438b7ea66a2dc8ac92cfbe309a0d24c898bbfac8c295.png)\n\n \n\nHow can we find the maximum possible value of $\\|A \\mathbf x\\|?$ Geometrically, this is equivalent to finding the semi-major axis of our ellipse.\n\n![](images/86/866052291b0d7a416988c0c42fa99eec1d1bfd3b1344cf3cf2eb0a09457ae38d.png)\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n- The value of $\\mathbf x$ that maximizes $||A\\mathbf x||$ also maximizes $||A\\mathbf x||^2.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf v$ is a column vector, then $||\\mathbf v||^2 = \\mathbf v^T \\mathbf v\\mathbin{:}$\n\nTherefore, we can re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNote that $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is a quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ Therefore, the maximum value of this quadratic form for $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1$ equals the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^TA\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )^{2}-36 & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )^{2} & =36 \\\\ 10-\\lambda  & =\\pm 6 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $16,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 16\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = \\sqrt{16} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the length of the semi-major axis of our ellipse equals $4.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163320 :tutorial/content "We've seen that for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/9c/9c11e176876a767543250d5b18038cae7b14a36a692a6f14474da6b4312a48b9.svg)\n\nthe eigenvalues of $A^TA$ are $\\lambda_1=16$ and $\\lambda_2=4.$ It's easy to show that the corresponding unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$ are \n\n![](images/7c/7c44b67b766ea8385f4d7780523ee560bd3e215a0cee42db9baf208da471ccdd.svg) \n\nThe singular values of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{16}=4, \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting all of this information together gives us the following diagram. \n\n![](images/23/238f4ae60c783dd786476a339f012ef1b4ca82bcac7aa3f3ef4445726938ef3a.png)\n\n \n\nNote that our ellipse's semi-major and semi-minor axes are $\\sigma_1 = 4$ and $\\sigma_2 =2,$ respectively." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162225 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
